Skip the F_GETLK probe for every "unix-excl" connection, read-only ones included.

unix-excl keeps the locks inside the process (bProcessLock), but a read-only connection never
takes the in-process lock, so unixCheckReservedLock still asked fcntl(F_GETLK). FATFS file locks
do nothing and F_GETLK leaves l_type F_WRLCK: the reserved lock always looked held, and a
read-only connection opened first after a reset ignored the hot journal and read the half-written
pages. Now it sees the journal and gets SQLITE_READONLY_ROLLBACK until a read-write connection
rolls it back.

diff --git a/sqlite3.c b/sqlite3.c
--- a/sqlite3.c
+++ b/sqlite3.c
@@ -42366,9 +42366,14 @@
   }
 
   /* Otherwise see if some other process holds it.
+  **
+  ** php-baremetal: with "unix-excl" there is no other process. A read-only
+  ** connection never sets bProcessLock, and on FATFS F_GETLK reports every
+  ** byte locked: the probe would hide a hot journal from it.
   */
 #ifndef __DJGPP__
-  if( !reserved && !pFile->pInode->bProcessLock ){
+  if( !reserved && !pFile->pInode->bProcessLock
+   && (pFile->ctrlFlags & UNIXFILE_EXCL)==0 ){
     struct flock lock;
     lock.l_whence = SEEK_SET;
     lock.l_start = RESERVED_BYTE;
